#{[{:activity/expected-seconds 1380.0 :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167732} :activity/id #uuid "d2daf8ec-962f-5cd4-9714-2cfebccb518d" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048878 :topic/id #uuid "c8e7edcb-eb86-52ac-9a3b-92e4b445b353" :topic/math-academy-id 3260} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167626 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166791 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166629 :question/id #uuid "b6135ab8-d329-5646-aeb7-dea8b38e7606" :question/math-academy-id "e-8420"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "f04980f8-35db-5cf4-8a4d-8e9f0f23fb1e" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049599}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167138} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167400} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186536872} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186536877} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186536879} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186536885}] :knowledge-point/title "Integrating a Product of Hyperbolic Functions"} :step/id #uuid "96a09359-527a-5e5b-b3ff-0751c279f524" :step/math-academy-id 631340 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167627}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167627 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166792 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166630 :question/id #uuid "555cf939-5320-5230-bcef-4828210fbc34" :question/math-academy-id "e-8421"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "e14a3673-7b90-5f81-b73a-c2894f07c561" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167401} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167402} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186536814} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186536840} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186536864} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186536894} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186536900}] :knowledge-point/title "Integrating a Quotient of Hyperbolic Functions"} :step/id #uuid "2b9ea8f0-27f6-52e0-95a8-a6c153a1617f" :step/math-academy-id 631343 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167628}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167628 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166793 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166631 :question/id #uuid "27ef207b-1337-5a3b-92cc-0d7a8b6bb13e" :question/math-academy-id "e-8422"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "f7fafc0a-8fff-5c9d-a55a-f25ce3c1d729" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050489}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167403} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167404} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186536826} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186536845} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186536849} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186536873} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186536899}] :knowledge-point/title "Computing a Hyperbolic Integral Using a Substitution"} :step/id #uuid "7dbc74d1-d41f-5d76-806c-c0b277105b07" :step/math-academy-id 631346} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167732 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186168117 :tutorial/content "It's possible to convert every trigonometric identity to a corresponding *hyperbolic* identity using **Osborn's rule.** \n\nTo apply Osborn's rule, we follow two steps:\n\n1\\. Replace every trigonometric function with its corresponding hyperbolic function.\n\n2\\. For any product of *two trigonometric sines*, change the sign of the corresponding product of hyperbolic sines.\n\nTo demonstrate, consider the Pythagorean trigonometric identity:\n\n$$\n\\newcommand{\\sech}{\\mathop{\\rm sech}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\csch}{\\mathop{\\rm csch}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\coth}{\\mathop{\\rm coth}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\arsinh}{\\mathop{\\rm arsinh}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\arcosh}{\\mathop{\\rm arcosh}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\artanh}{\\mathop{\\rm artanh}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\arsech}{\\mathop{\\rm arsech}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\arcsch}{\\mathop{\\rm arcsch}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\arcoth}{\\mathop{\\rm arcoth}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\cosh}{\\mathop{\\rm cosh}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\sinh}{\\mathop{\\rm sinh}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\csch}{\\mathop{\\rm csch}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\coth}{\\mathop{\\rm coth}\\nolimits}\n\\cos^2 x \\:{\\color{blue}{\\boldsymbol +}}\\: \\sin^2 x = 1\n$$\n\nNotice that this trigonometric identity contains a product of two sines. Therefore, to find the corresponding hyperbolic identity, we change each trigonometric function to its corresponding hyperbolic counterpart and change the sign in front of the corresponding product of sines. \n\nThus, the corresponding hyperbolic identity is\n\n$$\n\\cosh^2 x \\:{\\color{red}{\\boldsymbol -}}\\: \\sinh^2 x = 1.\n$$\n\n \n\nWe can check that this identity is indeed true using the definitions of the hyperbolic sine and cosine:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{cosh}^{2}⁡x-{sinh}^{2}⁡x & ={(\\frac{1}{2}({e}^{x}+{e}^{-x}))}^{2}-{(\\frac{1}{2}({e}^{x}-{e}^{-x}))}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}{({e}^{x}+{e}^{-x})}^{2}-\\frac{1}{4}{({e}^{x}-{e}^{-x})}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}({({e}^{x}+{e}^{-x})}^{2}-{({e}^{x}-{e}^{-x})}^{2}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}({e}^{2x}+2+{e}^{-2x}-({e}^{2x}-2+{e}^{-2x})) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}(\\cancel{{e}^{2x}}+2+\\cancel{{e}^{-2x}}-(\\cancel{{e}^{2x}}-2+\\cancel{{e}^{-2x}})) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}\\cdot 4 \\\\  & =1\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "b6c4b399-c2b4-5fcb-a5b8-60bb6d23c29a" :tutorial/math-academy-id 5552 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "4693d31f-c75b-551c-85f0-e6ea3040b97e" :step/math-academy-id 631334 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167804}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167761 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166872 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166725 :question/id #uuid "e05932ad-3ee7-5b8a-a0c9-8e8ac50990d0" :question/math-academy-id "e-9881"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "4c7ddaa7-f225-56f3-966a-f214152b1ad0" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048192} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048867} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050125}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167464} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167465} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186536817} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186536846} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186536878}] :knowledge-point/title "Deriving a Hyperbolic Identity Using Osborn's Rule"} :step/id #uuid "2f36f0be-0a1c-5d94-9a98-49a786e635b5" :step/math-academy-id 631336 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167803}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167803 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186168152 :tutorial/content "Suppose we want to calculate the integral\n\n$$\n\\newcommand{\\sech}{\\mathop{\\rm sech}\\nolimits}\n        \\newcommand{\\csch}{\\mathop{\\rm csch}\\nolimits}\n        \\newcommand{\\coth}{\\mathop{\\rm coth}\\nolimits}\n        \\newcommand{\\arsinh}{\\mathop{\\rm arsinh}\\nolimits}\n        \\newcommand{\\arcosh}{\\mathop{\\rm arcosh}\\nolimits}\n        \\newcommand{\\artanh}{\\mathop{\\rm artanh}\\nolimits}\n        \\newcommand{\\arsech}{\\mathop{\\rm arsech}\\nolimits}\n        \\newcommand{\\arcsch}{\\mathop{\\rm arcsch}\\nolimits}\n        \\newcommand{\\arcoth}{\\mathop{\\rm arcoth}\\nolimits}\n\n\\int \\dfrac{1}{\\sinh^2 x}\\,\\textrm{d}x.\n$$\n\nFirst, we note the following hyperbolic identity:\n\n$$\n\\csch{x} = \\dfrac{1}{\\sinh{x}}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write our integral as\n\n$$\n\\int \\dfrac{1}{\\sinh^2{x}} \\,\\textrm{d}x\n= \\int \\left(\\dfrac{1}{\\sinh{x}}\\right)^2 \\,\\textrm{d}x\n= \\int \\csch^2{x} \\,\\textrm{d}x.\n$$\n\nFrom here, we can see that this is just a standard hyperbolic integral:\n\n$$\n\\int \\dfrac{1}{\\sinh^2{x}}\\,\\textrm{d}x = \\int \\csch^2{x}\\,\\textrm{d}x = -\\coth{x} + C\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "f612c2c5-2457-59c4-899a-0847e3f278a5" :tutorial/math-academy-id 7913 :tutorial/title "Integration Using Hyperbolic Identities"} :step/id #uuid "02f143e3-76ee-5144-89d8-7142626764d4" :step/math-academy-id 631339 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167626}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167804 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186168153 :tutorial/content "When applying Osborn's rule, we must be aware that sometimes a product of two sines is \"hidden.\"\n\nTo demonstrate, consider the following trigonometric identity:\n\n$$\n\\newcommand{\\sech}{\\mathop{\\rm sech}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\csch}{\\mathop{\\rm csch}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\coth}{\\mathop{\\rm coth}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\arsinh}{\\mathop{\\rm arsinh}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\arcosh}{\\mathop{\\rm arcosh}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\artanh}{\\mathop{\\rm artanh}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\arsech}{\\mathop{\\rm arsech}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\arcsch}{\\mathop{\\rm arcsch}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\arcoth}{\\mathop{\\rm arcoth}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\cosh}{\\mathop{\\rm cosh}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\sinh}{\\mathop{\\rm sinh}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\csch}{\\mathop{\\rm csch}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\coth}{\\mathop{\\rm coth}\\nolimits}\n1 \\:{\\color{blue}{\\boldsymbol +}}\\: \\tan^2 x = \\sec^2 x\n$$\n\nAt first glance, it may seem that there is no product of sines, and we do not need to change any signs in the corresponding hyperbolic identity.\n\nHowever, recall that\n\n$$\n\\tan x = \\dfrac{\\sin x}{\\cos x}\n$$\n\nwhich implies that\n\n$$\n\\tan^2 x = \\dfrac{\\sin^2 x}{\\cos^2 x}.\n$$\n\nSo, there is a hidden product of sines within the $\\tan^2 x$ term, and we need to flip the sign in front of this term when writing down the corresponding hyperbolic identity.\n\nThus, the corresponding hyperbolic identity is\n\n$$\n1 \\:{\\color{red}{\\boldsymbol -}}\\: \\tanh^2 x = \\sech^2 x.\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "0cd7ebe1-9de5-5914-a1e9-4b88c94e5a0e" :tutorial/math-academy-id 7914 :tutorial/title "Osborn’s Rule in Cases Where a Product of Sines Is Hidden"} :step/id #uuid "25c4fda7-21cf-54ba-8f5a-d6ff1375cd9b" :step/math-academy-id 631335 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167761}}] :activity/title "Integration Using Basic Hyperbolic Identities" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186167026}]}
